            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The curse of sin, divine judgment, and the hardship resulting from the Fall.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 3:18\",\"Matthew 27:29\",\"Hebrews 6:8\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Worldly cares, anxieties, and the deceitfulness of wealth that choke spiritual growth.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 13:7\",\"Matthew 13:22\",\"Mark 4:18-19\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Wicked, worthless, or destructive people, including false prophets and corrupt rulers.\",\"basis\":[\"Judges 9:14-15\",\"2 Samuel 23:6\",\"Matthew 7:16\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Personal afflictions, trials, or spiritual harassment (e.g., a thorn in the flesh).\",\"basis\":[\"2 Corinthians 12:7\",\"Numbers 33:55\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Thorns and thistles are universally recognized in Christian theology as symbols of the curse of sin introduced in Genesis. Christ's crown of thorns is widely interpreted as his physical bearing of the curse on humanity's behalf. In parables, brambles frequently represent either destructive leaders (Jotham's parable) or worldly distractions (Parable of the Sower).\"}",
